<!--
  https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/fundamentals/package-validation/diagnostic-ids

  Every entry here is a deliberate, reviewed decision. A suppression that nobody can explain is a
  silenced bug, so do not add one without recording why below, and delete one the moment the reason
  stops holding. Regenerating this file with -p:ApiCompatGenerateSuppressionFile=true rewrites it and
  DISCARDS these comments - regenerate into a scratch copy and merge by hand instead.

  1. IEnumerableExtensions.Index<TSource> - present on the net8.0 leg, absent on net10.0.
     Intentional and pre-existing: the method is a polyfill guarded by #if !NET9_0_OR_GREATER,
     because .NET 9 added Enumerable.Index with an identical signature. On net10.0 the consumer gets
     the BCL's, so nothing is lost. Compiling it on both legs would collide exactly the way
     ArrayExtensions.Reverse did (see below) - this guard is the pattern that avoids that, not a
     defect. Invisible until the package multi-targeted, because a single-TFM package has no second
     leg to differ from.

  2. ArrayExtensions.Reverse (six overloads) - removed against the 0.7.0 baseline.
     Intentional break, taken in the 1.0.0 major. .NET 10 added
     System.Linq.Enumerable.Reverse<TSource>(TSource[]), which made our same-shaped extension
     ambiguous (CS0121) for any consumer importing both namespaces; [OverloadResolutionPriority]
     does not help, as it is not consulted across declaring types. The family was renamed to
     Reversed, which also states that the source is not mutated. These suppressions cover only the
     removal of the old names - the new ones are validated normally, as is the rest of the surface.
     Drop these entries once the baseline moves past 1.0.0, since by then the old names will not be
     in the baseline to miss.
-->
